<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_4_4">


<title>Power Functions: Fractional and Negative Integral Exponents</title>

<introduction>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
            First objective.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
        Second objective.
            </p>
        </li> 
        <li>
            <p>
            Third objective.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</objectives>


<p>
    In the previous section, we considered power functions <m>f(x) = kx^p</m> in the case when <m>p</m> is a positive integer. When <m>p</m> is a negative integer, power functions behave quite differently.
</p>


</introduction>





<subsection>
    <title>Negative Integral Exponents</title>

    <p>
        Consider a power function <md>f(x) = kx^p</md> in the case when the exponent <m>p</m> is a negative integer. The behavior and graphs of such functions are very different from those of power functions with positive integral exponents.
    </p>

    <p>
        A negative exponent <m>x^{-m}</m> means the reciprocal <m>\frac{1}{x^m}</m>. So now the independent variable is in the denominator. The domain of a power function in this case is no longer the set of all real numbers as <m>x</m> cannot be <m>0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        We now look more closely at these graphs.
    </p>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
Which of the functions below are power functions? Rewrite those which are in standard form <m>y = kx^p</m>. Identify the coefficient and the exponent.
</p>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{3x^2}{2x^3}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{-\pi}{x^4}</m>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \frac{70}{3^x}</m>
</p>
</li>

    </ol></p>
</statement>

<solution>

<p><ol>
<li>
<p>
    The function <m>f(x)</m> is a power function. Using Rules of Exponents from Section 4.1, we can rewrite: <md>f(x) = \frac{3x^2}{2x^3} = \frac{3}{2x} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{x} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot x^{-1}.</md> The coefficient is <m>k = \frac{3}{2}</m> and the exponent is <m>p = -1</m>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>g(x)</m> is a power function: <md>g(x) = \frac{-\pi}{x^4} = -\pi x^{-4}.</md> The coefficient is <m>k = -\pi</m> and the exponent is <m>p = -4</m>.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
    <m>h(x)</m> is not a power function. Using rules of exponents for arbitrary exponents, we can rewrite: <md>h(x) = \frac{70}{3^x} = 70 \cdot 3^{-x}.</md> The function <m>h(x) = 70 \cdot 3^{-x}</m> is not a power function. Indeed, the base of the power expression <m>3^{-x}</m> is constant at <m>3</m>, meanwhile the exponent <m>-x</m> varies.
</p>
</li>

</ol></p>
</solution>
</example>

<example>
<statement>
<p>
    Body mass index (BMI) is an easy screening method for weight category <mdash/> underweight, healthy weight, overweight, and obesity. BMI is calculated as follows: <md>\text{BMI} = \frac{\text{(weight in lb)} \cdot 703}{(\text{height in inches})^2}</md>
</p>

<p>
    A person who weighs 170 lb may be underweight or obese<mdash/>it depends on the person's height. Let <m>I(h)</m> denote body mass index of a person who weighs 170 lb and whose height is <m>h</m>. Then, according to the formula, <md>I(h) = \frac{170 \cdot 703}{h^2}</md> Note that with the weight fixed at 170 lb, body mass index is a power function of height: <m>\displaystyle I = 119510 \cdot h^{-2}</m>. (In terms of proportionality, BMI is inversely proportional to the square of height.) The obese category is defined as BMI of 30.0 or above. The normal weight category corresponds to BMI between 18.5 and 24.9.
</p>

<p>
    Find the height <m>h</m> at and below which a person weighing 170 lb is obese.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
    We want to find <m>h</m> such that <m>I(h) = 30</m>: <md>\frac{170 \cdot 703}{h^2} = 30</md> After multiplying both sides by <m>h^2</m> and dividing by 30, then <md>h^2 = \frac{170 \cdot 703}{30}</md> which gives: <md>h = \pm\sqrt{\frac{170 \cdot 703}{30}}</md> Since <m>h</m> in our problem has to be positive, the solution is: <md>h = \sqrt{\frac{170 \cdot 703}{30}} \approx 63</md> At the height <m>h = 63</m> inches and below a person weighing 170 lb is obese.
</p>

<p>
    Note that when <m>h</m> increases <m>I(h)</m> decreases as the denominator of the expression <m>\displaystyle \frac{170 \cdot 703}{h^2}</m> becomes larger. On the other hand, when <m>h</m> gets smaller, <m>I(h)</m> gets larger. Hence, every person shorter than <m>63</m> inches who weighs 170 lb is also obese.
</p>
</solution>
</example>




</subsection>





<subsection>
    <title>Graphs of Power Functions: Negative Integral Exponents</title>

    <p>
        Graphs of power functions <m>y = kx^p</m> with exponents <m>p</m> that are negative integers have a different shape depending on whether <m>p</m> is even or <m>p</m> is odd.
    </p>

    <p>
        <term>Odd Negative Exponents</term>
    </p>

    <p>
        We begin with the case <m>k = 1</m> and <m>p = -1</m>. The graph of the function <m>\displaystyle y = x^{-1} = \frac{1}{x}</m> looks as follows:
    </p>


<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-6,ymax=6,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={-6,...,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:6] {1/x} node[above right, pos=0.6] {$y=x^{-1}$};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1] {1/x}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

    <p>
        Observe that when <m>x</m> gets larger and larger, say: <md>x = 1, \hspace{2pt} 2, \hspace{2pt} 3, \hspace{2pt} 4, \hspace{2pt} 5, \hspace{2pt} \dots 1000 \hspace{2pt} \dots,</md> the values <m>\displaystyle y = \frac{1}{x}</m> become very close to 0. Indeed, they are: <md>y = 1, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{2}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{3}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{4}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{5}, \hspace{2pt} \dots\frac{1}{1000}\dots.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
        In terms of the graph, this behavior translates to the graph getting very close, arbitrarily close, to the <m>x</m>-axis. We say that the <m>x</m>-axis, or equivalently the horizontal line <m>y = 0</m>, is a <term>horizontal asymptote</term> of the function <m>y = \dfrac{1}{x}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Observe that as <m>x</m> is getting close to 0 from the right, the values are becoming very large. We test a few positive inputs close to <m>0</m>: <md>x = \frac{1}{2}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{3}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{4}, \hspace{2pt} \frac{1}{5}, \hspace{2pt} \dots \frac{1}{1000}\dots</md> The corresponding values <m>\displaystyle y = \frac{1}{x}</m> are: <md>y = 2, \hspace{2pt} 3, \hspace{2pt} 4, \hspace{2pt} 5, \hspace{2pt} \dots 1000 \hspace{2pt} \dots</md> In terms of the graph, this behavior translates to the graph getting very close to the <m>y</m>-axis with the values of the function becoming arbitrarily large. We say that the <m>y</m>-axis, or equivalently the vertical line <m>x = 0</m>, is a <term>vertical asymptote</term> of the function <m>\displaystyle y = \frac{1}{x}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The portion of the graph corresponding to negative inputs <m>x</m>, is symmetric about the origin to the portion of the graph corresponding to positive inputs. Indeed, when <m>x</m> changes sign <m>y = \frac{1}{x}</m> changes sign: <md>\frac{1}{-x} = -\frac{1}{x}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Graphs of other power functions <m>y = kx^p</m> in which the exponent <m>p</m> is an odd negative integer have a similar shape to the graph of <m>\displaystyle y = \frac{1}{x}</m>. For example:
    </p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-6,ymax=6,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={-6,...,6},
minor tick={},
legend pos = north east,
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:6] {1/x};
\addlegendentry{$1/x$};
\addplot[domain=0.5:6,red] {1/x^3};
\addlegendentry{$1/x^3$};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1] {1/x};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.5,red] {1/x^3}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

    <p>
        As always, the coefficient <m>k</m> in <m>y = kx^p</m> stretches or shrinks the graph of <m>y = x^p</m> vertically. Additionally, if <m>k</m> is negative, the graph is reflected about the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>

<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="bottom top">
<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_3">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_3_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-7,ymax=7,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={-7,...,7},
minor tick={},
legend pos = north east,
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:6] {1/x};
\addlegendentry{$1/x$};
\addplot[domain=0.1:6,red] {2/x};
\addlegendentry{$2/x$};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1] {1/x};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1,red] {2/x}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_4">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_4_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-7,ymax=7,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={-7,...,7},
minor tick={},
legend pos = north east,
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:6] {1/x};
\addlegendentry{$1/x$};
\addplot[domain=0.1:6,red] {-1/x};
\addlegendentry{$-1/x$};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1] {1/x};
\addplot[domain=-6:-0.1,red] {-1/x}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</sidebyside>



    <p>
        <term>Even Negative Exponents</term>
    </p>

    <p>
        When <m>p</m> is a negative even integer, the graph of <m>y = x^p</m> is entirely above the <m>x</m>-axis and it is symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis, since <m>(-x)^p = x^p</m> when <m>p</m> is even.
    </p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_5">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_5_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-0.5,ymax=6,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={0,...,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:3] {1/x^2};
\addlegendentry{$1/x^2$};
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.1] {1/x^2}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

    <p>
        The graphs of the functions <m>y = x^p</m> for other even negative integers <m>p</m> have a similar shape:
    </p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_6">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_6_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-0.5,ymax=6,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={0,...,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:3] {1/x^2};
\addlegendentry{$1/x^2$};
\addplot[domain=0.5:3,red] {1/x^4};
\addlegendentry{$1/x^4$};
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.1] {1/x^2}; 
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.5,red] {1/x^4}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

    <p>
        The <m>x</m>-axis is still a horizontal asymptote and the <m>y</m>-axis is a vertical asymptote. The coefficient <m>k</m> is responsible for vertical scaling and a reflection about the <m>x</m>-axis if <m>k</m> is negative. For example:
    </p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_4_7">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="4_4_7_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-6,ymax=6,
xtick={-3,...,3},
ytick={-6,...,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:3] {1/x^2};
\addlegendentry{$1/x^2$};
\addplot[domain=0.5:3,red] {4/x^2};
\addlegendentry{$4/x^2$};
\addplot[domain=0.5:3,orange] {-4/x^2};
\addlegendentry{$-4/x^2$};
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.1] {1/x^2}; 
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.5,red] {4/x^2}; 
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.5,orange] {-4/x^2}; 
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<p>
    <term>Summary</term>
</p>

<p>
    Here is a summary of how graphs of power functions look for negative integer exponents <m>p</m>, even and odd, and for coefficients <m>k</m> positive and negative.
</p>


<sidebyside widths="20% 40% 40%" margins="0% 0%"
    valigns="middle middle middle">
<p><m>f(x)=kx^p</m></p>
<p><m>p</m> even and negative</p>
<p><m>p</m> odd and negative</p>
</sidebyside>

